@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Modal Sub-Flows and Results") {
        Not every modal deserves a coordinator. This tutorial draws the line,
        then builds the case that does: a picker sheet with its own navigation
        that hands a value back to whoever presented it.

        You will keep a one-screen confirmation native, promote a multi-step
        picker to a coordinator, configure the sheet from the presenter,
        dismiss from both sides, and await a result back from it.

        @Image(source: "03-settings-sheet", alt: "A settings screen presented as a modal sheet with a Done button.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Choose the Right Modal") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The rule is short: **if the modal contains navigation, make it a
            coordinator and present it. If it is a single screen, use SwiftUI's
            native modifier.** A native sheet costs no destination case and no
            coordinator boundary.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                A removal confirmation is one screen with no navigation — keep
                it native, with local `@State` in the view. This is the one place
                a view may own modal state, because the modal is not part of the
                flow.

                @Code(name: "FavoritesView.swift", file: "modals-01-native-sheet.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                The planet picker is different: it has a list *and* a
                "custom planet" screen pushed on top. That is a flow, so it
                becomes a ``FlowCoordinatable`` of its own.

                > This is also the answer to "my sheet needs a
                `NavigationStack`". Do not nest one inside a flow's views —
                cross a coordinator boundary and get a fresh stack legitimately.

                @Code(name: "PlanetPickerCoordinator.swift", file: "modals-02-subflow-coordinator.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Present the Sub-Flow") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The presenter decides *how* the modal looks. The presented screens
            stay ignorant of their presentation, so the same route can be a
            medium sheet in one place and a full-screen cover in another.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add a `picker()` route returning `any Coordinatable`, then
                present it with detents. `route(to:)` always pushes; modals go
                exclusively through `present(_:as:)`.

                @Code(name: "FavoritesCoordinator.swift", file: "modals-03-present.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Add a second modal the user must not dismiss — a sync overlay —
                and close it from the presenter with `dismissModal()`.

                > `dismissModal()` removes only the topmost modal, fires its
                `onDismiss` exactly once, and no-ops when nothing is presented.
                It is the only way to close a *view-only* modal
                programmatically, since there is no child coordinator to call
                `dismissCoordinator()` on.

                @Code(name: "FavoritesCoordinator.swift", file: "modals-04-dismissal.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Get a Result Back") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The presenter never observes the presented coordinator's state. It
            awaits the presentation, and the picker hands a value back as it
            closes: the result arrives on the next line, cancellation is free,
            and the route carries no closure — which is also what keeps it
            eligible for <doc:StateRestoration>.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                **Awaited result.** `await present(_:awaiting:)` suspends until
                the sheet closes and returns what the picker handed back. Any
                other dismissal — a swipe, `dismissModal()`, a plain
                `dismissCoordinator()` — resumes with `nil`, so the cancel path
                needs no extra code.

                @Code(name: "FavoritesCoordinator.swift", file: "modals-05-awaiting.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                On the child side, `dismissCoordinator(returning:)` delivers the
                value and closes in one call; a plain `dismissCoordinator()`
                resumes the presenter with `nil`.

                > `dismissCoordinator()` removes the whole coordinator from its
                parent — for a sheet, that closes the sheet. `pop()` would only
                remove the top screen inside it.

                @Code(name: "PlanetPickerCoordinator.swift", file: "modals-06-returning.swift")
            }

        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Wait Without a Result") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Sometimes you only need "continue once it's gone" — an onboarding
            cover, a permission primer, a picker that writes into shared state
            itself.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                `presentAndWait(_:as:)` suspends until the modal is dismissed,
                and `routeAndWait(to:)` does the same for a pushed screen. Both
                resume exactly once, however the destination leaves.

                @Code(name: "FavoritesCoordinator.swift", file: "modals-09-present-and-wait.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                **Build and run.** Confirm a removal through the native sheet,
                add a planet through the picker sub-flow — pushing the custom
                screen inside the sheet without disturbing the list behind it —
                and watch the awaited call resume with your choice.

                Next: <doc:TestingCoordinators> pins all of this down in tests,
                including the cancellation path.
            }
        }
    }
}
